		<FBProgram name="ACS880In">
			<STSource>(*
***********************************************************************************************
   FUNCTION BLOCK - ACS880In
***********************************************************************************************
  Description:      ABB ACS880 VSD Interface In Object
  Date Changed:	    27/05/2026
  Change Comments:  Updated DATA_IN_5 to be Torque (01.10) in comments and oSts.Trq to read from DATA_IN_5 instead of Act_Torque
  Revision:    	    2.01

***********************************************************************************************
  Copyright Rio Tinto Pty Ltd, 2026.
***********************************************************************************************

***********************************************************************************************
Description:
***********************************************************************************************
The object carries out the status mapping of data from the ACS880 VSD drive over Ethernet/IP.
The input map is based on ESD profile 'ABB Drives 102/152 Set Speed and Set Torque plus Drive Parameters',
with the first 2 words fixed and the remaining words set within the FEIP-21 module. The EDS
file required for this interface is provided by ABB with file name
"FEIP21_filerev1_1.EDS".

The data map is as follows:
  WORDS         DESCRIPTION
- SW          - Drive State (Fixed) (Note: STO Active Status is mapped into User Bit 2)
- Act_Speed   - Speed Actual (Fixed)
- Act_Torque  - Torque Actual (Fixed)
- DATA_IN_1   - Speed Status (06.19)
- DATA_IN_2   - DI1-6 Status (10.01)
- DATA_IN_3   - Brake Ctrl Status (44.01)
- DATA_IN_4   - Current (01.07 - INT)
- DATA_IN_5   - Torque (01.10) [Discovered on WR that the field bus parameters must be continuous to avoid comms error]
- DATA_IN_6   - Frequency (01.06 - INT)
- DATA_IN_7   - Power (01.14 - INT)
- DATA_IN_8   - Cabinet Temp (01.31 - INT)
- DATA_IN_9   - Tripping Fault (04.01 - INT)
- DATA_IN_10  - Future


The ACS880 drive must have the following parameters set:
NOTE: Values are for Fieldbus Adapter (FBA) A. This object does not work with FBA B.
NOTE: All parameters are 16bit.
06.32	06.18.07 (STO)		Assigns STO Active status to user bit 13 of Status
				Word. Value must not be inverted.
46.01				Speed Scaling Factor
46.02	50			Frequency  Scaling Factor
46.03	100			Torque Scaling Factor
46.04	1000			Power Scaling Factor
50.02	*As Required*		FBA A Comms Loss Function
50.03	*As Required*		FBA A Comms Loss Timeout
51.02	101 (EIP ABB PRO)	Protocol/Profile
51.04	*As Required*		IP Configuration
51.05	*As Required*		IP Address 1 (XXX.xxx.xxx.xxx)
51.06	*As Required*		IP Address 2 (xxx.XXX.xxx.xxx)
51.07	*As Required*		IP Address 3 (xxx.xxx.XXX.xxx)
51.08	*As Required*		IP Address 4 (xxx.xxx.xxx.XXX)
51.09	*As Required*		Subnet CIDR
51.10	*As Required*		Gateway Address 1 (XXX.xxx.xxx.xxx)
51.11	*As Required*		Gateway Address 2 (xxx.XXX.xxx.xxx)
51.12	*As Required*		Gateway Address 3 (xxx.xxx.XXX.xxx)
51.13	*As Required*		Gateway Address 4 (xxx.xxx.xxx.XXX)
52.01	06.19			DATA IN 1 Value
52.02	10.01			DATA IN 2 Value
52.03	44.01			DATA IN 3 Value
52.04	01.07			DATA IN 4 Value
52.05	06.19			DATA IN 5 Value
52.06	01.06			DATA IN 6 Value
52.07	01.14			DATA IN 7 Value
52.08	01.31			DATA IN 8 Value
52.09	04.01			DATA IN 9 Value
52.10	*Future*		DATA IN 10 Value

NOTE: After changing any settings, refresh the drive by setting 96.07 - 'Parameter Save
Manually' to 1. And, if changing parameters in groups 51, 52 and 53, refresh the FEIP-21 by
setting 51.27 – 'FBA A PAR Refresh' to 1.


The minimum speed (iMinSpd) input is used for scaling the speed setpoint and corresponds to
0% speed. The maximum speed (iMaxSpd) input is used for scaling the speed setpoint and
corresponds to 100% speed. For example, if the speed setpoint is 50%, iMaxSpd is 1000 rpm and
iMinSpd is 500 rpm, the drive will operate at 750rpm. The maximum speed (iMaxSpd) should match
or be lower than the drive maximum speed parameter (30.12). The drive minimum speed parameter
(30.11) should be the negative of the maximum speed (Parameter 30.12) for reversing drives,
and should be 0 for non-reversing drives.

The main power supply healthy status must be tagged to the iPwrOK2 input. If the VSD contains
a contactor, the iPwrOK2 inputs acts as a request to the VSD to close the contactor.

The ACS880 requires some time after the main circuit breaker (iPwrOK2) is closed to power up.
If the drive is unable to power up, the fault bit (oFlt) is set. The drive start fault time
(iSttFltTm) delays the fault until the drive has completed trying to power up. A time of 2s is
appropriate in most cases.

The start available output (oSttAvl) and the lock out time (oLckOutTm) are provisioned for
future use. The start available output should be included in the drive start permissive
string.

The brake lift function (oBrkLft) of this block only works when brake control is configured in
the drive. Refer to drive parameter 44.06. The brake should be lifted immediately after
oBrkLft becomes active. Brake lift confirmation should be done outside the DFB is applicable.

This DFB is to be used in conjunction with the ACS880Out block. The ACS880Out block is used
to send drive parameters to the drive.

***********************************************************************************************

***********************************************************************************************
Revision History:
***********************************************************************************************
2.01	BCY	27/05/2026	Updated DATA_IN_5 to be Torque (01.10) in comments and oSts.Trq to read from DATA_IN_5 instead of Act_Torque
2.00	BCY	20/03/2026	Removed Free0 from ACS880RawInDDT to suit I/O Scanning mode change from Legacy to Enhanced and updated comments to reflect new FEIP module
1.00	NJA	25/06/2020	Initial Release

***********************************************************************************************


*)


(* CONSTANTS *)
(* Hard code constant values for calculations. These must match the parameters in
the drive. These are provisioned to be read from the drive in the future *)
ioExtCfg.SpdFac	:= iSpdPar;		(* Speed Scaling (46.01) *)
ioExtCfg.FrqFac	:= 50.0;		(* Frequency Scaling (46.02) *)
ioExtCfg.TrqFac	:= 100.0;		(* Torque Scaling (46.03) *)
ioExtCfg.PwrFac	:= 1000.0;		(* Power Scaling (46.04) *)

(* SPEED SCALING FACTOR *)
(* To allow transparency when running a drive lower than speed scaling parameter but this
is still to register a different value, such as 100%, provision a speed scaling factor into
the block *)
IF iSpdFac &lt;= 0.0 THEN
	SpdFactor := 1.0;
ELSE
	SpdFactor := iSpdFac;
END_IF;

(* COMMUNICATION STATUS *)
(* The PLC clears the data before setting the LnkOK statusus inactive. Check
if the data from the VSD is all zeros to mask any VSD faults *)
NoDat := (iDatDDT.SW = 0) AND (iDatDDT.DATA_IN_1 = 0) AND (iDatDDT.DATA_IN_2 = 0);
(* Check for a communications link between the PLC and the VSD controller *)
TON_1 (IN := (iPLMOK AND iLnkOK AND NOT NoDat), PT := t#2s, Q =&gt; oDatOK);

(* A communications fault is only raised provided the unit has had enough time to 
initialise after power up *)
TON_2 (IN := (iPLMOK AND iPwrOK), PT := t#90s, Q =&gt; Powered);
oComFlt := Powered AND NOT iLnkOK;

(* Set the PowerUp in Progress status for diagnostics *)
oPwrUp := (iPLMOK AND iPwrOK) AND NOT Powered AND NOT oDatOK;

(* Any communication fault is automatically reset once communication is
re-established. This prevents the internal faulted status within the unit
remaining latched and requiring an additional reset. *)
ioExtCfg.ComRst := iPLMOK AND iLnkOK AND NOT ComStsLst;
ComStsLst := iPLMOK AND iLnkOK;


(* Write the drive min/max speed value to the configuration data *)
ioExtCfg.MinSpd	:= iMinSpd;
ioExtCfg.MaxSpd	:= iMaxSpd;


(* Write the main circuit breaker close status to the interface data *)
(* Delay the main circuit breaker closed status by the start fault delay to give
the ACS880 time to respond *)
TON_3 (IN := (iPwrOK2 AND oDatOK), PT := iSttFltTm, Q =&gt; ioExtCfg.PwrOK2);


(* Create a working DDT for the drive data, which upon invalid data is set to zero *)
IF oDatOK THEN
	DatRaw := iDatDDT;
ELSE
	DatRaw.SW		:= 0;
	DatRaw.Act_Speed	:= 0;
	DatRaw.Act_Torque	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_1	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_2	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_3	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_4	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_5	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_6	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_7	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_8	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_9	:= 0;
	DatRaw.DATA_IN_10	:= 0;
END_IF;

(* DATA MAPPING *)	
(* Break out the bytes into the applicable statuses and scale/condition them *)
PwrOn			:= DatRaw.SW.0;								(* Ready to Power On (Ready to Close Main Contactor) *)
Ready			:= DatRaw.SW.1;								(* Ready to Operate (Run) *)
OprEn			:= DatRaw.SW.2;								(* Operation Enabled (Running But Ramping May Not be Enabled) *)
Fault			:= DatRaw.SW.3;								(* Fault *)
Stop2			:= DatRaw.SW.4;								(* Off 2 Healthy *)
Stop3			:= DatRaw.SW.5;								(* Off 3 Healthy *)
DrvInh			:= DatRaw.SW.6;								(* Switch On Inhibit Active (Activated when Off2 or Off3 are Set Unhealthy) *)
Warning			:= DatRaw.SW.7;								(* Alarm *)
AtRef			:= DatRaw.SW.8;								(* At Setpoint *)
Remote			:= DatRaw.SW.9;								(* Drive Control Remote *)
SpdHi			:= DatRaw.SW.10;							(* Above Setpoint *)
STOOn			:= DatRaw.SW.13;							(* Safety Torque Off Active (Unhealthy) *)
ModFlt			:= DatRaw.SW.15;							(* FBA Module Fault *)

oSts.SpdEng		:= ABS((W_INT_REAL(DatRaw.Act_Speed) * ioExtCfg.SpdFac) / 20000.0);	(* Engineering Speed (rpm) (Speed is scaled by Parameter 46.01) *)

oSts.Trq 		:= ABS((W_INT_REAL(DatRaw.DATA_IN_5) * ioExtCfg.TrqFac) / 10000.0);	(* Torque (%) (Torque is scaled by Parameter 46.03) *)

Running			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_1.0;							(* Running at zero speed *)
oRunFwd			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_1.1;							(* Running Forward *)
oRunRev			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_1.2;							(* Running Reverse *)

oCh1			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_2.0;							(* Input Channel 1 *)
oCh2			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_2.1;							(* Input Channel 2 *)
oCh3			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_2.2;							(* Input Channel 3 *)
oCh4			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_2.3;							(* Input Channel 4 *)
oCh5			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_2.4;							(* Input Channel 5 *)
oCh6			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_2.5;							(* Input Channel 6 *)
oILOK			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_2.15;							(* Harwired Interlock Healthy *)

oLftReq			:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_3.0;							(* Brake Lift Required *)
LftCtlEn		:= DatRaw.DATA_IN_3.4;							(* Brake Control Enabled *)


oSts.IAvg		:=      W_INT_REAL(DatRaw.DATA_IN_4);					(* Average Current (A) *)
oSts.Frq 		:= ABS((W_INT_REAL(DatRaw.DATA_IN_6) * ioExtCfg.FrqFac) / 20000.0);	(* Frequency (Hz)  (Frequency is scaled by Parameter 46.02) *)
oSts.Pwr 		:= ABS((W_INT_REAL(DatRaw.DATA_IN_7) * ioExtCfg.PwrFac) / 10000.0);	(* Power (kW)  (Power is scaled by Parameter 46.04) *)
oSts.Tmp 		:= ABS( W_INT_REAL(DatRaw.DATA_IN_8));					(* Temperature (°C) *)
oSts.FltID		:=      WORD_TO_DINT(DatRaw.DATA_IN_9);					(* Fault Code *)



(* Compute Speed (%) *)
(* Ensure speed does not go negative and divide by zero check *)
IF ((ioExtCfg.MaxSpd - ioExtCfg.MinSpd) &lt;&gt; 0.0) AND (oSts.SpdEng &gt; ioExtCfg.MinSpd) THEN
	oSts.Spd := SpdFactor * (oSts.SpdEng - ioExtCfg.MinSpd) * 100.0 / (ioExtCfg.MaxSpd - ioExtCfg.MinSpd);
ELSE
	oSts.Spd := 0.0;
END_IF;


(* If the ACS880 control panel is in local mode, then flag this status as no remote
operation is possible. *)
oLocSts := NOT Remote AND oDatOK;


(* Power up the main power supply for the drive
The main circuit breaker must be closed before powering up the drive.
If the VSD contains an internal contactor, it will closed when this signal is set.
If the drive faults, the drive will inhibit powering up. To reset the drive, this signal
must be set to zero.
This value is written to the drive by the ACS880PBOut block. *)
ioExtCfg.DrvPwrOn := oDatOK AND NOT DrvInh AND PwrOn AND iPwrOK2;



(* If the ACS880 uses the Safety Torque Off (STO) function, then the drive fault status
must be masked when the STO is activated.
When the drive STO becomes unhealthy, the drive is faulted and the STO Active status is
set high. When the drive STO input becomes healthy, the STO Active status is set low but
the drive fault status remains latched until the drive is reset through the iRst pin of
the ACS880Out DFB.
If the STO becomes unhealthy, generate a STO fault which is latched up until the drive
is reset. *)
STOFltSL := oDatOK AND (STOOn OR (STOFltSL AND Fault));
(* Delay the oSTOFlt becoming healthy for a moment to continue masking the drive Ready
And PwrOn statuses while they become healthy. *)
TOF_0(IN := STOFltSL, PT := T#500ms, Q =&gt; oSTOFlt);


(* Determine the status of the STO input for display only. The input is healthy when comms
are healthy and the STO Mask status is off. *)
oSTOOK := oDatOK AND NOT STOOn;



(* The ACS880 is healthy provided communications is established and the unit is not faulted.
This bit is expected to be used for interlocking within code. *)
oOK :=	oDatOK AND
	PwrOn AND
	Ready AND
	NOT Fault AND
	NOT ModFlt AND
	NOT oLocSts AND
	NOT oSTOFlt AND
	oILOK;

oFlt :=	oDatOK AND
	(	ModFlt OR
		NOT oSTOFlt AND
		(	Fault OR
			(NOT PwrOn AND ioExtCfg.PwrOK2) OR
			(NOT Ready AND ioExtCfg.PwrOK2)
		)
	);



(* ReStart Available *)
(* Provision for future limited restart logic *)
oSttAvl := TRUE;
oLckOutTm := 0;



(* Map the parameters to the block outputs *)
oIAvg		:= oSts.IAvg;
oTrq		:= oSts.Trq;
oSpd		:= oSts.Spd;
oSpdEng		:= oSts.SpdEng;


(* Map the parameters to the output DFB DDT *)
ioExtCfg.DatOK	:= oDatOK;
ioExtCfg.OK	:= oOK;
ioExtCfg.STOOK	:= oSTOOK;
</STSource>
		</FBProgram>
	</FBSource>
